<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      One package carries the whole telemetry surface: the client seam (the
      controlled domain model and the facade binding) and the panels built on
      it. The epic's package layout names exactly one telemetry entry, and
      splitting the panels into a second project would have to invent an area
      name for them, because "Telemetry" is already this one - the plugin
      naming rule is Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Plugins.<Area>, and there is only
      one telemetry area. The seam has exactly one consumer, so the split would
      buy nothing and cost a package.
    -->
    <RootNamespace>Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Plugins.Telemetry</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Plugins.Telemetry</AssemblyName>
    <PackageId>Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Plugins.Telemetry</PackageId>
    <Version>9.4.0</Version>
    <Description>The Telemetry plugin for the Orleans.Lattice Explorer: the client seam over the telemetry gRPC binding plus the time-series panels built on it. Panels are composed entirely from the server-authored catalogue - the titles, units, semantics and parameter bounds the cluster publishes - so no query id, label, or limit is hard-coded in the client and no control anywhere accepts free-text query input. Every result renders the tenant scope the facade actually pinned, and says so when a requested visibility was degraded. The same panels also fill the My Tenant area's metrics section, pinned to the caller's own tenant. Registered with AddExplorerTelemetry(), AddExplorerTelemetryPlugin() and AddExplorerTelemetryMyTenantSection(); served by a referencing app at _content/Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Plugins.Telemetry.</Description>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/NSTA1/Orleans.Lattice</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/NSTA1/Orleans.Lattice</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageReadmeFile>NUGET_README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="NUGET_README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DynamicProxyGenAssembly2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A sibling plugin project references its own facade's gRPC binding directly,
    exactly as Access -> api.auth.grpc, Backup -> api.backup.grpc and
    Schema -> api.schema.grpc do. Explorer.Core keeps its single
    api.state.grpc reference untouched. No dependency is taken on the shared UI
    library, so a head that ships no telemetry surface pays for none of it.

    The My Tenant reference is deliberately in this direction. The tenant
    metrics section is one adapter naming a component this package already
    owns, so it belongs here; pointing the arrow the other way would put a
    gRPC binding into My Tenant, whose whole shape is that it reaches the
    cluster through the tenancy seam and references no wire contract at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\DesignSystem\Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.DesignSystem.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Abstractions\Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Plugins.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MyTenant\Orleans.Lattice.Explorer.Plugins.MyTenant.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\lattice.api.telemetry.grpc\Orleans.Lattice.Api.Telemetry.Grpc.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Grpc.Net.Client" Version="2.83.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Serialization" Version="10.2.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
